00:00:43.315 --> 00:00:50.189
One of the spectacular details of animals in our&nbsp;
world is just how varied their colors can be.

00:00:50.189 --> 00:00:57.161
When you look at birds, for example, you’ll see&nbsp;
everything from mundane grays to iridescent blues.

00:00:57.161 --> 00:01:04.185
Birds some times craft those colors using melanosomes,&nbsp;
an organelle that holds the pigment melanin.

00:01:04.185 --> 00:01:07.260
And nothing about that seems&nbsp;
particularly unique to birds.

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:13.125
We have melanosomes, and we use&nbsp;
melanin to make our various skin colors.

00:01:13.125 --> 00:01:18.600
So then why don’t we ever shine with&nbsp;
the same iridescence as birds?

00:01:21.437 --> 00:01:24.312
Well, we could just blame it on light.

00:01:24.312 --> 00:01:27.749
It’s always doing weird things,&nbsp;
playing tricks with what we see.

00:01:27.749 --> 00:01:32.848
So let’s just say that light is doing&nbsp;
something funny and call it a day.

00:01:33.923 --> 00:01:36.546
Except that’s very unsatisfying, isn’t it?

00:01:36.546 --> 00:01:39.360
If light has so many tricks up its sleeves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:46.377
that only makes me want to know more about the&nbsp;
specific tricks it’s playing with bird feathers.

00:01:46.377 --> 00:01:52.119
And that means we’re going to have to start by&nbsp;
talking about color, specifically pigments.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:01.800
Pigments are the compounds that color&nbsp;
everything from our paints to our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:06.553
cosmetics, and we see them all&nbsp;
the time in the microcosmos.

00:02:06.553 --> 00:02:13.200
Perhaps most common of all of course would be 
the color&nbsp;green, found in cyanobacteria and euglenoids&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:19.163
and plenty of other photosynthetic organisms&nbsp;
thanks to the pigment molecule chlorophyll.

00:02:19.163 --> 00:02:24.836
When white light hits the chlorophyll, the&nbsp;
molecule absorbs the red and blue wavelengths.

00:02:24.836 --> 00:02:29.293
But it also reflects the green 
wavelengths back at us.

00:02:29.293 --> 00:02:31.845
Which is how pigments work:

00:02:31.845 --> 00:02:36.180
they are molecules that absorb certain&nbsp;
wavelengths, and in turn they reflect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:42.870
a particular wavelength of light back at
us, which to our eyes, then looks like color.

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:47.760
So when we look at these Haematococcus and see&nbsp;
a mix of green and red, what we’re actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:53.467
seeing is a mixture of pigment molecules&nbsp;
reflecting different wavelengths of light.

00:02:53.467 --> 00:02:55.680
There’s the chlorophyll reflecting green,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.680 --> 00:03:00.258
and the astaxanthin pigment&nbsp;
molecule reflecting red light back at us.

00:03:00.258 --> 00:03:04.561
And so the lesson we take from pigments&nbsp;
is that light doesn’t just shine.

00:03:04.561 --> 00:03:07.531
It reflects and absorbs.

00:03:07.531 --> 00:03:12.871
But it also does more than that.

00:03:12.871 --> 00:03:15.749
That became very clear to Robert Hooke,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.749 --> 00:03:22.726
who in 1665 published what is probably one of&nbsp;
the most important texts in microscopy history:

00:03:22.726 --> 00:03:28.920
Micrographia, or Some Physiological Descriptions&nbsp;
of Minute Bodies Made by Magnifying Glasses:

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:32.100
With Observations and Inquiries Thereupon.

00:03:35.946 --> 00:03:42.120
This work contained detailed descriptions and&nbsp;
illustrations of objects Hooke examined under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:47.843
the microscope, including the feathers Hooke&nbsp;
found from peacocks, ducks, and other birds.

00:03:47.843 --> 00:03:50.280
Hooke compared the structures he saw on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:54.745
the iridescent peacock feathers&nbsp;
to pearls, which, as he wrote quote:

00:03:54.745 --> 00:04:01.365
do not onely reflect a very brisk light, but&nbsp;
tinge that light in a most curious manner;

00:04:01.365 --> 00:04:07.845
and by means of various positions, in respect&nbsp;
of the light, they reflect back now one colour,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.845 --> 00:04:11.760
and then another, and those most vividly.

00:04:13.086 --> 00:04:18.240
As scientists began to better understand&nbsp;
what light is and how it interacts with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:22.234
the world around it, they were able&nbsp;
to understand what was happening.

00:04:22.234 --> 00:04:26.559
The light was not getting absorbed&nbsp;
and reflected by a pigment.

00:04:26.559 --> 00:04:31.365
It was interacting with the physical&nbsp;
structures within the feather,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.365 --> 00:04:37.273
refracting and diffracting and scattering&nbsp;
and whatever else the light can do.

00:04:37.273 --> 00:04:41.604
The result, to our eyes, is structural color:

00:04:41.604 --> 00:04:47.473
a color whose wavelength is the result of&nbsp;
the structures the light was moving through.

00:04:47.473 --> 00:04:51.960
And for some structural colors, the&nbsp;
angle of the viewer relative to what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:58.234
they’re looking at will change the&nbsp;
color itself, and this produces iridescence.

00:05:00.273 --> 00:05:03.585
You can think of the difference&nbsp;
between pigments and structural color&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.585 --> 00:05:08.135
as kind of like the difference between&nbsp;
looking at a crayon and a rainbow.

00:05:08.135 --> 00:05:11.800
When we see a rainbow, we’re&nbsp;
not looking at colored water.

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:16.860
We’re seeing light refract and reflect&nbsp;
and disperse in those water droplets.

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:20.220
Except at the end of the day, a&nbsp;
rainbow is a function of the rain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:25.653
and sunshine and your eyes, making it an&nbsp;
illusory object that you can of course never touch

00:05:25.653 --> 00:05:27.780
or find the end of.

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:30.720
Feathers, on the other hand, are very tangible.

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:36.166
And the structures they use to craft their&nbsp;
colors are very present and observable.

00:05:36.166 --> 00:05:40.500
But before we dive too deep into&nbsp;
how feathers use structural color,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:45.060
we should note that birds can and do use&nbsp;
pigments as well for their coloring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:50.227
mixing carotenoids, melanins, and&nbsp;
porphyrines for their various hues.

00:05:50.227 --> 00:05:55.137
But birds can also expand on that range,&nbsp;
using structural colors.

00:05:55.137 --> 00:05:59.484
The blues you see in these&nbsp;
feathers here are structural color.

00:05:59.484 --> 00:06:03.180
Blue pigment is unusually&nbsp;
difficult to find in nature.

00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:08.220
So when the color does appear, like in these&nbsp;
feathers or in the wings of a butterfly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:11.583
it is often from structural color.

00:06:11.583 --> 00:06:17.520
Now the structures that make that happen&nbsp;
are located in the barbules, those tiny,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:21.962
interlocking threads that make&nbsp;
a feather feel, well you know like, feathery.

00:06:21.962 --> 00:06:25.200
Inside the barbules are keratin and melanosomes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:29.676
those melanin-containing organelles we&nbsp;
mentioned in the beginning of the episode.

00:06:29.676 --> 00:06:33.540
And it's the arrangement of these&nbsp;
melanosomes within the keratin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:39.722
that create the structures that will in&nbsp;
turn determine the color of the feather.

00:06:39.722 --> 00:06:43.544
Now a lot of animals, including again us, 
have melanosomes.

00:06:43.544 --> 00:06:48.345
But birds are unique because&nbsp;
their melanosomes can be hollow.

00:06:48.345 --> 00:06:54.580
And the more hollow the melanosome, scientists&nbsp;
have found, the more iridescent the feathers.

00:06:54.580 --> 00:07:00.000
And if they produce thinner melanosomes that&nbsp;
are shaped more like a flat plate than a rod,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:03.145
the iridescence will only increase.

00:07:03.145 --> 00:07:06.300
These features of the melanosomes are essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:12.960
changing the way that light moves through&nbsp;
the feather, crafting a different color.

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:18.366
Scientists are still learning about how these&nbsp;
structural colors have evolved in feathers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.366 --> 00:07:24.643
and why there’s so much variety in&nbsp;color 
and iridescence across different birds species.

00:07:24.643 --> 00:07:28.386
These are structures that once contained pigments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.386 --> 00:07:37.458
but evolved to contain nothing, because being&nbsp;
empty somehow made even more brilliant colors.

00:07:37.458 --> 00:07:44.640
It is a wild leap to make, but of course,&nbsp;
evolution doesn’t think, if color is produced,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:50.576
and it confers an advantage, evolution&nbsp;
doesn’t care or know how this happened,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.576 --> 00:07:55.500
the photons that would have existed&nbsp;whether 
or not life ever evolved&nbsp;in our universe&nbsp;

00:07:55.500 --> 00:08:00.640
doing a marvelous dance in the empty&nbsp;
pigment organelles of a birds feather,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.640 --> 00:08:08.068
it evolved without anyone ever knowing&nbsp;
how it worked, until us, until now.

